WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:03.830
Thank you to Ancestry for supporting PBS. 

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:08.760
If you are a fan of science fiction a name
you should be familiar with is Octavia E. Butler 

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:14.559
Especially if you watched our
telly award-winning Afro-Futurism video. Starring me!

00:00:14.559 --> 00:00:19.890
One of the most prolific and important Black
authors in the genre, Butler’s storytelling

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:24.880
pushed the boundaries of what Black people
were allowed to be in science fiction.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:30.810
Today we will be highlighting the Grand Dame
herself, and how her novels were important, and

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:40.769
sometimes, oddly predictive. Like super freaking predictive.

00:00:40.769 --> 00:00:45.960
Octavia Estelle Butler was born in Pasadena,
California in 1947.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:50.159
Her father died when she was a toddler and
she was raised by her grandmother and mother

00:00:50.159 --> 00:00:54.410
in what she would recall as a very strict Baptist
environment.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:01:00.630
That religious experience would come up often
in her work, especially in the Parable series.

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:06.950
Butler would describe herself as awkward,
introverted, and painfully shy which led to

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:12.570
her being picked on by her fellow classmates growing up. Which, ya know, every nerdy black girl story. Preaching to the choir there, Octavia.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:20.380
Later, she would describe herself in the following
self-deprecating way:

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.180
“Who am I?

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:29.800
... A pessimist if I'm not careful, a feminist,
a Black, a former Baptist, an oil-and-water

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:36.910
combination of ambition, laziness, insecurity,
certainty, and drive.”

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:41.909
Honestly that should be my tinder profile. Thumbs up if you agree.

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:46.490
Despite being pushed by her mother to find
a more secure job, Butler pursued her writing goals.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:52.330
She participated in the Open Door Workshop
of the Writers Guild of America West, a program

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:58.960
designed to specifically mentor minority writers,
and ended up impressing science-fiction writer Harlan Ellison.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:04.840
He encouraged her to attend the six-week Clarion
Science Fiction Writers Workshop in Pennsylvania

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:08.700
where she met, fellow Black sci-fi writer
Samuel R. Delany.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:14.370
After selling some short stories in 1971 she
would spend the following five years writing

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:16.540
on what would become the Patternist series.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:24.560
It was with the release of that series that
she finally became a full-time author, the dream, and

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:30.840
subsequently in 1979 she published what is
probably her most famous novel, Kindred.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:36.230
Kindred tells the story of an African American
woman named Dana Franklin, who, for reasons

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:42.590
we never really find out, is pulled back in time
to a pre-Civil War Maryland plantation.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:49.550
On her first journey there she meets her ancestors:
Alice Greenwood, a Black freewoman, and Rufus

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:55.630
Weylin, a white planter who eventually forces
Alice into slavery and sexual servitude.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:03:02.319
Dana’s trips into the past range from one
moment to years and each trip does substantial

00:03:02.319 --> 00:03:05.350
harm to her mind and spirit.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:12.239
Especially having to deal with the harsh reality
that her existence is part of this dark legacy of sexual violence.

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:18.900
It would be one of the many times Butler would
address the legacy of slavery in the science fiction element.

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:27.190
Madhu Dubey, professor of English and Afro-American Studies at the University of Illinois at Chicago, said of Butler.

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:32.380
“In common with other contemporary African
American writers, Butler persistently revisits

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:37.709
slavery in order to challenge the redemptive
accounts of US racial history that began to

00:03:37.709 --> 00:03:41.019
gain sway in the decades following the civil
rights movement.

00:03:41.019 --> 00:03:46.690
.... Yet, in a distinct departure from the
neo-slave narrative genre, the signifier of

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:53.190
"slavery" in Butler's novels mutates to refer
to a range of abusive practices, such as sexual

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:58.900
trafficking, reproductive oppression, and
debt bondage, which are not reducible to race;

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:04.360
even when race does operate as a central axis
of inequality in Butler's novels of enslavement,

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:11.390
it far exceeds the binary (black-white) logic
bequeathed by the institution of chattel slavery”

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:15.870
These themes are most obvious in Kindred but
pop up throughout her work, such as in the

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:20.969
Patternist series, where an ancient human
with the ability to body snatch people basically

00:04:20.969 --> 00:04:28.389
breeds his own descendants like cattle in
the pursuit of creating a new post-human species, which, okay.

00:04:28.389 --> 00:04:39.529
Or the Xenogenesis trilogy, wherein after
the Earth blows itself up in nuclear armageddon, possible,

00:04:39.529 --> 00:04:45.120
some aliens show up and force what’s left
of humanity to breed with each other.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.990
And also the aliens. Don't know why, but okay.

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:56.759
Tragically, Butler’s life was cut short
when she died at the age of 58 in 2006.

00:04:56.759 --> 00:05:01.520
The novel Fledgling, a story about a vampire
that was changed very young, was published posthumously.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:07.409
Octavia E. Butler is what we would call a
certified genius and her work has resonated

00:05:07.409 --> 00:05:13.169
so powerfully because of themes and imagery
she was able to bring together.

00:05:13.169 --> 00:05:20.409
Yet, when people talk about sci-fi and Blackness
together, it is treated as some anomaly.

00:05:20.409 --> 00:05:26.219
Something that Butler herself would talk about
in her essay “Birth of a Writer” first published in Essence:

00:05:26.219 --> 00:05:33.442
“There seems to be an unwritten rule, hurtful
and at odds with the realities of American culture.

00:05:33.442 --> 00:05:40.509
It says you aren’t supposed to wonder whether
as a Black person, a Black woman, you really

00:05:40.509 --> 00:05:46.499
might be inferior—not quite bright enough,
not quite quick enough, not quite good enough

00:05:46.499 --> 00:05:49.069
to do the things you want to do.

00:05:49.069 --> 00:05:54.289
... Act tough and confident and don’t talk
about your doubts.

00:05:54.289 --> 00:05:59.580
If you never deal with them, you may never
get rid of them, but no matter.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:02.870
Fake everyone out. Even yourself.”

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:07.029
While all of her novels and short stories
have a lot we could discuss, we decided to

00:06:07.029 --> 00:06:13.199
focus on the Parable novels because there
is nothing like living in your own dystopia

00:06:13.199 --> 00:06:18.000
to make things come into focus - if you get what I mean.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:27.899
Released in 1993, Parable of the Sower takes
place in the distant future of 2024,

00:06:27.899 --> 00:06:33.590
when society in the United States and our
favorite unholy trinity of climate change,

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:45.389
wealth inequality, and corporate greed has
ravaged the nation leaving in in a state of pure dystopia.

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:52.009
We start this journey with the main character,
Lauren, an African American teenager,

00:06:52.009 --> 00:06:54.949
who details everything in a journal.

00:06:54.949 --> 00:06:59.590
She has a condition called “hyper-empathy"
where she feels the emotions of others, due

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:03.619
to the side effects of a drug her late mother
took while pregnant.

00:07:03.619 --> 00:07:07.550
Lauren lives with her father, step-mother,
and three half brothers in the remains of

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:11.060
a gated community in California.

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:15.409
She and her community struggle to maintain
any sense of civilization against the Mad

00:07:15.409 --> 00:07:25.479
Max world outside where rape, torture, violence,
and pure lawlessness are allowed to take place.

00:07:25.479 --> 00:07:32.039
Police officers and firefighters exploit their
positions taking brides for-profit and do very little to

00:07:32.039 --> 00:07:34.529
actually serve any common good.

00:07:34.529 --> 00:07:40.550
Lauren's father, a Baptist pastor, has been
trying to keep their community civilized and

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:51.360
collaborative through democratic socialism (cough) Excuse me, that was my propaganda showing.
I mean, sharing of resources and investing

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:55.589
in mutual aid for each other - totally not democratic socialism.

00:07:55.589 --> 00:08:00.469
Still, Lauren can tell this is only a temporary
thing.

00:08:00.469 --> 00:08:05.229
In response to the darkness surrounding her,
she starts the religion Earthseed, based around

00:08:05.229 --> 00:08:07.550
the concept “God is Change.”

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:12.129
“God is Change. This is the literal truth . God cannot

00:08:12.129 --> 00:08:18.089
be resisted or stopped , but can be shaped
and focused. This means God is not to be

00:08:18.089 --> 00:08:24.279
prayed to. Prayers only help the person doing
the praying , and then , only if they strengthen

00:08:24.279 --> 00:08:31.179
and focus that person’s resolve. If they’re
used that way, they can help us in our only

00:08:31.179 --> 00:08:37.280
real relationship with God. They help us
to shape God and to accept and work with the

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:44.600
shapes that God imposes on us. God is power, and in the end, God prevails.”

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:48.600
After her family and most of her community
are destroyed and burnt down, Lauren begins

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:54.420
to travel North and along the way builds a
congregation through mutual aid and assistance.

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:59.245
The second novel, Parable of the Talents,
takes place five years after the end the previous novel.

00:08:59.245 --> 00:09:06.360
America has become controlled by a Christian
fundamentalist denomination called "Christian

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:11.980
America" led by President Andrew Steele Jarret, a totally normal name.

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:16.040
Despite the optimistic ending of the first
book, we see Lauren and her followers deal

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:18.390
with an even darker America.

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:23.070
John Blair Gamber, Professor of English at
Utah State University, says of this theme:

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:27.780
“...while Sower appears to have a utopian
conclusion, Talents irrefutably shows it to

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:31.460
be dystopian—a failure of misguided idealism.

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:37.580
Specifically, the implicit critique of antiurbanism
and flight that comprise the past-oral impulse

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:42.100
and aesthetic we see in Sower becomes explicit
in Talents.

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:46.800
The primary failure of this impulse comes
in the protagonist’s inability to recognize

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:52.530
her role within a wide-reaching community
or her fundamental connection to local and

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:56.510
distant structures of power, oppression, and
marginalization.”

00:09:56.510 --> 00:10:04.390
President Jarret’s presidency is filled
with death and torture as he ...if you can believe this, wants to

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:10.050
restore American power on the global stage
and actually, literally uses the slogan

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:16.110
"Make America Great Again" [screams into the
void]

00:10:16.110 --> 00:10:22.310
Jarret brings back slavery with shock collars
being used to control people and restarts

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:26.020
witch hunts in order to wipe out non-Christian
religions.

00:10:26.020 --> 00:10:31.280
Virtual reality headsets called "Dreamasks"
also become very popular since they enable

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:35.150
wearers to escape their harsh reality and
okay … now this is messing with me.

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:39.840
Who copied this timeline into the nexus and can you control "D" it?

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:45.250
Earthseed, as a faith, continues to spread
and develop, despite the violence that is

00:10:45.250 --> 00:10:55.103
depicted and eventually after one-term, President
Jarret is not reelected. Yaayyy. Okay that also tracks. That's fine. That's good.

00:10:55.103 --> 00:11:01.244
Humanity then begins its expansion into the
stars carrying with it the ideas that Lauren started.

00:11:01.244 --> 00:11:12.000
At the end of the novel, she watches the first
of Earth’s starships leave called the … Christopher Columbus.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:14.690
Says Lauren in the book’s conclusion:

00:11:14.690 --> 00:11:17.310
“I object to the name.

00:11:17.310 --> 00:11:21.040
This ship is not about a shortcut to riches
and empire.

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:28.480
It’s not about snatching up slaves and gold
and presenting them to some European monarch.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:30.810
But one can’t win every battle.

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:32.180
One must know which battles to fight.”

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:33.180
Parable of the Talents (408)

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:38.340
Even the utopian society she is constructing
starts off with a little crumb of colonization,

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:45.300
addressing that nothing can truly detach
humanity from that sin, especially when tied to the United States.

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:52.014
Scholar Jim Miller in “Post-Apocalyptic
Hoping: Octavia Butler’s Dystopian/Utopian Vision said:

00:11:52.014 --> 00:11:57.100
“As an African-American woman writing within
a largely white women's tradition, her work

00:11:57.100 --> 00:12:01.870
often questions the assumptions shared by
many white feminist utopian writers.

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:07.470
Butler is also far more class-conscious than
many other utopian feminist science-fiction writers.

00:12:07.470 --> 00:12:14.430
Thus, her largely dystopian fictions challenge
not only patriarchal myths, but also capitalist

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:20.070
myths, racist myths, and feminist-utopian
myths.”

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:26.790
Butler herself doesn’t seem to care much
for utopias and said as much during an interview in

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:30.130
1988 with Larry McCaffery and Jim McMenamin:

00:12:30.130 --> 00:12:32.030
“I find utopias ridiculous.

00:12:32.030 --> 00:12:40.580
We're not going to have a perfect society
until we get a few perfect humans, and that seems unlikely.

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:46.930
Besides, any true utopia would almost certainly
be incredibly boring, and it would probably

00:12:46.930 --> 00:12:55.340
be so overspecialized that any change we might
introduce would probably destroy the whole system.

00:12:55.340 --> 00:12:59.900
As bad as we humans are sometimes, I have
a feeling that we'll never have that problem

00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:01.730
with the current system.”

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:06.560
These themes are woven throughout Butler’s
body of work, which asks complex and often

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:12.040
challenging questions about the nature of
autonomy and how it interacts with community,

00:13:12.040 --> 00:13:14.710
and usually with no clear answers.

00:13:14.710 --> 00:13:20.570
But Butler’s work uses science fiction to
advocate for a more just world, despite knowing

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:27.380
full well that the road to get there won’t
be easy, nor, like Lauren not personally leaving

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:32.380
Earth to see humanity spread beyond the stars,
would she live to see it.

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:37.950
Jim Miller writes that, “the ideal for Butler
seems to be a society which respects individual

00:13:37.950 --> 00:13:42.920
differences, but not at the expense of the
greater good of the society as a whole.

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:48.890
The achievement of the collective good should
not require the neglect of individual autonomy,

00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:54.200
nor should the price of freedom be the "inevitable"
deprivation of the weakest among us.”

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:59.490
Octavia E. Butler is a name that has only
grown in esteem in the past few years as Black

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:03.020
authorship in science fiction and fantasy
has only expanded.

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:09.310
Regardless of how you feel about her work,
because boy can it get dark, she laid the

00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:14.120
foundation through which contemporary Afrofuturists
stand upon.

00:14:14.120 --> 00:14:23.540
She was bold and brave in her Blackness and
the way in which her stories feel so modern is a testament to her own genius and creativity.

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:31.320
Taken much too soon there is no telling how
much she could have done if she were alive

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:37.771
today, still writing and still pushing Black
people to the stars.

00:14:37.771 --> 00:14:43.905
Thank you to Ancestry for supporting PBS. Was that your great grandmother keeping the family together? 

00:14:43.905 --> 00:14:50.796
Was that your great grandfather paving the way for change? Did they brave mother nature, and walk away stronger?

00:14:50.796 --> 00:14:56.562
 Did they face the unknown with resolve – and triumph? There is strength in every family story. 

00:14:56.562 --> 00:15:01.545
